Subject: [PATCH] usb: hub: skip the connect debounce on the OTG root port
Upstream-Status: Pending

First car capture with kernel patch 0046 v3 on a NAC Wave 2, two role
switches in one boot. The second one is the first complete accessory
handshake this hardware has done with CatPlay:

  [10.838596] force_mode(host)
  [10.940082] _dwc2_hcd_start: after hcd_reinit HPRT0=00021401 (PWR=1)  <- +101ms, head unit already pulled up
  [10.940131] Port Connect Detected
  [11.112518] Port Enable Changed (now 1)                               <- reset released, +172ms
  [11.185283] usb 1-1: new high-speed USB device number 2 using dwc2
  [11.211305] usb 1-1: New USB device found, idVendor=15a2, idProduct=1234
  [11.211384] usb 1-1: Manufacturer: Continental
  [11.260506] iPhone: role-switch accessory probe succeeded for if=usb0

The first one got the head unit on the bus just the same, but lost it
before the reset was ever asserted:

  [ 1.528982] _dwc2_hcd_start: after hcd_reinit HPRT0=00021401 (PWR=1)
  [ 1.529066] Port Connect Detected
  [ 1.784007] hprt0 sample 2/24: HPRT0=00021000 [PWR ]                 <- gone, no reset in between

So once the head unit has swapped roles it sits as a device waiting for
a bus reset and gives up somewhere between ~200ms and ~250ms after
pulling up. Of the 172ms the successful attempt took from connect to
reset release, 50ms is the reset itself (dwc2_hcd_hub_control()) and
~120ms is the hub thread doing nothing in hub_port_connect():
hub_port_debounce_be_stable() waits for 100ms of stable connection in
25ms steps before it lets enumeration begin. Under boot load, as in the
first attempt, that plus scheduling latency is longer than the head
unit waits.

That debounce exists for mechanical contacts. Here the connection is
protocol-controlled: the peer switched roles at our request and pulled
up D+ on a cable that was carrying traffic a moment earlier. Patch 0032
already skips the hub_activate() debounce on this port for the same
reason; this skips the per-connect one too, using the same
hub_is_b_host_otg_port() test (which, with 0032's TODO, is true for
every root port - there is exactly one on this SoC). The port status is
re-read once so a device that vanished in the meantime is still seen,
and hub_port_init() handles a disconnect during reset as before.

Expected effect: reset asserted within a few ms of "Port Connect
Detected", "New USB device found" ~150ms after it instead of ~270ms,
and the first attempt after boot succeeding as well.

Written against the letux-6.18.y tree at a3d00c045d89 with 0032
applied. Not yet compiled or run.
---
 drivers/usb/core/hub.c | 41 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---------
 1 file changed, 32 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/usb/core/hub.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/core/hub.c
@@ -5427,16 +5427,40 @@
 
 	if (portchange & (USB_PORT_STAT_C_CONNECTION |
 				USB_PORT_STAT_C_ENABLE)) {
-		status = hub_port_debounce_be_stable(hub, port1);
-		if (status < 0) {
-			if (status != -ENODEV &&
-				port1 != unreliable_port &&
-				printk_ratelimit())
-				dev_err(&port_dev->dev, "connect-debounce failed\n");
-			portstatus &= ~USB_PORT_STAT_CONNECTION;
-			unreliable_port = port1;
+		if (hub_is_b_host_otg_port(hdev, port1)) {
+			/*
+			 * Protocol-controlled connection on the OTG root port:
+			 * the peer switched roles on our request and pulled up
+			 * D+ on the same cable that was stable a moment ago.
+			 * There is no contact bounce to wait out, and the peer
+			 * is now a device waiting for a bus reset with a short
+			 * fuse. Take the current status and move on; a real
+			 * disconnect is caught by hub_port_init() anyway.
+			 */
+			u16 pc;
+
+			usb_clear_port_feature(hub->hdev, port1,
+					       USB_PORT_FEAT_C_CONNECTION);
+			status = usb_hub_port_status(hub, port1, &portstatus,
+						     &pc);
+			if (status < 0)
+				portstatus &= ~USB_PORT_STAT_CONNECTION;
+			else
+				dev_info(&port_dev->dev,
+					 "OTG root port: connect debounce skipped, status %04x\n",
+					 portstatus);
 		} else {
-			portstatus = status;
+			status = hub_port_debounce_be_stable(hub, port1);
+			if (status < 0) {
+				if (status != -ENODEV &&
+					port1 != unreliable_port &&
+					printk_ratelimit())
+					dev_err(&port_dev->dev, "connect-debounce failed\n");
+				portstatus &= ~USB_PORT_STAT_CONNECTION;
+				unreliable_port = port1;
+			} else {
+				portstatus = status;
+			}
 		}
 	}
 
